WB.L("new-coke-1985", "business", "beginner", "New Coke: the taste test that backfired", "In 1985 Coca-Cola replaced its famous recipe with one that beat the old drink in blind taste tests. Within three months the company was begging forgiveness.",
  [
    ["A giant under pressure", "Coca-Cola was still the market leader in the early 1980s, but its lead was slipping year after year.",
      "By the early 1980s Coca-Cola had a problem it could not shake. Its share of the American soft drink market had been sliding for years, while its rival Pepsi kept gaining. Pepsi was aimed squarely at younger drinkers, and from 1975 it ran a brilliant advertising campaign called the Pepsi Challenge. Ordinary shoppers took a sip from two unmarked cups and picked their favourite, and on camera they often picked Pepsi. Coca-Cola's own researchers ran their own blind tests and got the same uncomfortable answer. Under its new chief executive, Roberto Goizueta, the company decided to do something that had once seemed unthinkable: it would reformulate the drink itself, a recipe that had been treated as almost sacred for nearly a century."],
    ["Project Kansas", "The new formula was tested on a huge scale, and it won.",
      "The secret effort to develop and test a new recipe was code-named Project Kansas. Chemists produced a sweeter, smoother cola, and the company put it through an enormous programme of taste testing, reported at around 200,000 tests. In blind comparisons the new formula beat both the original Coke and Pepsi. On the numbers, the decision looked safe, even obvious. If people preferred the taste, surely they would welcome it. On 23 April 1985 Coca-Cola held a press conference in New York and announced that the old formula was being retired and replaced by what the public quickly called New Coke. The launch was one of the most publicised product changes in business history, and almost everyone in America heard about it within days."],
    ["The backlash", "Customers reacted not as tasters but as people who had lost something that belonged to them.",
      "The reaction caught the company completely off guard. Phone lines at Coca-Cola's Atlanta headquarters were flooded with thousands of angry calls, and sacks of letters arrived from people who felt personally betrayed. Some customers rushed to stockpile the remaining cases of the original drink. In Seattle, a man named Gay Mullins started a protest group called the Old Cola Drinkers of America and attracted wide media coverage. Comedians and newspaper columnists piled on. Much of the anger was not really about flavour at all. Many of the people complaining had never compared the two drinks carefully. They were upset that something tied to childhood, family and national identity had been taken away without anyone asking them first."],
    ["What the research missed", "The tests measured which drink people preferred in a sip, not what the brand meant to them.",
      "Looking back, the flaw in the research is easy to see. Blind taste tests answered a narrow question: which liquid do you prefer right now, in a small sample? They did not ask how people would feel if the old drink disappeared for good. One popular explanation adds that sweeter drinks tend to win quick sip tests, even when people would not choose them for a whole can, though how much that mattered here is still argued about. Some of the company's own focus groups had hinted at trouble, with a minority of participants reacting angrily to the idea of losing the original, but the strength of that feeling was underestimated. The lesson for any business is that customers buy meaning and habit as well as features."],
    ["The return of Coca-Cola Classic", "Just 79 days after the launch, the original formula came back.",
      "By early July the company had seen enough. On 11 July 1985 Coca-Cola announced that the original formula would return under the name Coca-Cola Classic, and the news led television bulletins across the country. Company president Donald Keough told reporters that some critics would say Coca-Cola had made a marketing mistake and some cynics would say it had planned the whole thing, and that the truth was the company was not that dumb and not that smart. For a while both drinks were sold side by side. Classic quickly outsold its replacement by a wide margin. New Coke was later renamed Coke II and lingered in a few markets for years before quietly disappearing from shelves."],
    ["Lessons that lasted", "New Coke shows that a brand's value lives partly in customers' heads, and that admitting a mistake fast can limit the damage.",
      "Was New Coke a disaster? In the short term it was an embarrassment, but the story has an odd twist. The uproar reminded Americans how attached they were to the original drink, and after Classic returned, Coca-Cola's sales recovered and its lead over Pepsi strengthened. The company did not plan this, but it did one thing well: once it understood its mistake, it reversed course quickly and publicly instead of defending the decision for years. Business schools still use the case to teach several lessons. Data can be precise and still answer the wrong question. Loyal customers can feel ownership of a product they do not legally own. And humility, shown early, is often cheaper than stubbornness."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What was the Pepsi Challenge?", ["A blind taste test campaign where shoppers chose between two unmarked colas", "A competition to design Pepsi's new logo", "A price war in which Pepsi sold cans below cost", "A sponsorship deal with American sports leagues"], 0, "From 1975 Pepsi filmed ordinary people picking their favourite of two unmarked drinks, and many picked Pepsi, which rattled Coca-Cola."],
    [1, "Why did replacing the formula look like a safe decision at the time?", ["The original recipe had become too expensive to make", "The new formula had beaten both old Coke and Pepsi in large blind taste tests", "Government regulators had banned an ingredient", "Pepsi had agreed to stop its advertising"], 1, "Around 200,000 reported taste tests favoured the new recipe, so the numbers seemed to point clearly to change."],
    [2, "What was most of the public anger really about?", ["The higher price of New Coke", "Smaller can sizes", "Losing something familiar and meaningful without being asked", "A health scare about the new ingredients"], 2, "Many complainers had not carefully compared the drinks; they were upset that a symbol tied to memory and identity had been taken away."],
    [3, "What was the key weakness in Coca-Cola's research?", ["It only tested people outside the United States", "It used too few participants to be reliable", "It was carried out by Pepsi's researchers", "It measured taste preference in a sip, not how people would feel about losing the original"], 3, "The tests answered a narrow question about flavour and missed the emotional attachment customers had to the original brand."],
    [5, "Which lesson does the New Coke story best illustrate?", ["Admitting a mistake quickly and publicly can limit the damage", "Taste tests should never be used in business", "Customers always prefer sweeter products", "Brands matter less than price"], 0, "Coca-Cola reversed course within 79 days, and its sales recovered; acting fast on a clear mistake helped it avoid lasting harm."]
  ],
  { added: "2026-10-26" });

WB.L("tylenol-crisis-1982", "business", "intermediate", "The Tylenol murders and the art of crisis response", "In 1982 seven people in Chicago died after taking poisoned painkillers. How one company responded became the textbook example of handling a crisis.",
  [
    ["Seven deaths in Chicago", "In late 1982 someone laced Extra-Strength Tylenol capsules with cyanide and put them back on shop shelves.",
      "On 29 September 1982 a twelve-year-old girl in a Chicago suburb took an Extra-Strength Tylenol capsule for a cold and died within hours. Over the next few days six more people in the Chicago area died in the same way, including three members of one family. Investigators soon found the link: capsules of Tylenol, the best-selling painkiller in the United States, had been filled with potassium cyanide. The bottles had come from different factories, which suggested the poison had not been added during manufacturing. Instead, someone appeared to have taken bottles from shops, tampered with them and returned them to the shelves. Panic spread across the country, and copycat tampering incidents followed in the weeks after."],
    ["A company in the spotlight", "Johnson and Johnson was not to blame, but its most valuable brand was suddenly linked with death.",
      "Tylenol was made by McNeil Consumer Products, a subsidiary of the healthcare giant Johnson and Johnson. Before the poisonings it was a huge success, holding roughly a third of the American market for over-the-counter painkillers. Almost overnight, that market share collapsed, with estimates suggesting it fell to under ten per cent. Marketing experts publicly predicted the brand would never recover, and some advised the company to retire the name altogether. The company faced a hard set of choices. It had not caused the crime, and the tampering seemed limited to one region. A narrow, legalistic response would have been cheaper in the short term. The question was whether protecting the public or protecting the balance sheet would come first."],
    ["Putting the public first", "The company recalled about 31 million bottles nationwide, at a cost of around 100 million US dollars.",
      "Led by chairman James Burke, Johnson and Johnson chose the expensive path. It worked closely with police, the Food and Drug Administration and the media, and it warned the public not to take any Tylenol capsules. Advertising for the product was halted. Then the company recalled Tylenol capsules from shops across the entire United States, around 31 million bottles, even though the poisoned ones had been found only around Chicago. It offered to swap customers' capsules for tablets. The cost was enormous, but the message was clear: the company would rather lose money than risk another life. Burke later said that the company's credo, a statement of values written in the 1940s that put customers first, guided the decisions."],
    ["Fixing the packaging", "Tylenol returned with tamper-evident packaging that soon became standard across the industry.",
      "The company did not stop at the recall. Within about two months it relaunched Tylenol capsules in new triple-sealed packaging: a glued box, a plastic seal around the neck and a foil seal over the mouth of the bottle. These features did not make tampering impossible, but they made it much easier to see. Sales representatives visited doctors and pharmacists, and the company offered discount coupons to win customers back. In 1983 the United States passed a federal law making tampering with consumer products a crime, and tamper-evident seals spread across food, drink and medicine. The foil seal you peel off a new jar today is partly a legacy of the 1982 poisonings."],
    ["A mystery never solved", "Despite decades of investigation, nobody has ever been charged with the murders.",
      "The criminal side of the story never found closure. A man named James Lewis sent Johnson and Johnson a letter demanding one million dollars to stop the killings. He was caught and convicted of extortion, and spent years in prison, but he was never charged with the poisonings themselves and always denied them. He died in 2023. Investigators reopened the case several times and tested new evidence as forensic science improved, yet the murders remain officially unsolved. For the families, this has meant decades without answers. For businesses, the case was a stark reminder that some crises begin entirely outside a company's control, and the only thing it truly controls is how it responds."],
    ["What crisis response teaches", "Speed, honesty and visible concern for people tend to rebuild trust better than defensiveness.",
      "Within about a year Tylenol had regained much of its lost market share, a recovery few experts had thought possible. The case is still taught as a model of crisis management, although some scholars note that the company was helped by being an obvious victim rather than a culprit. Several principles stand out. Act fast, even before you have every fact. Put public safety ahead of short-term profit, and be seen doing so. Communicate openly with authorities and the media rather than hiding. And fix the underlying weakness so the problem cannot easily happen again. Companies that later stumbled in a crisis were often criticised for doing the opposite: delaying, minimising and protecting themselves first."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why did investigators believe the poison was added after manufacturing?", ["A worker confessed", "The poisoned bottles came from different factories", "The bottles were all sold online", "The capsules were made overseas"], 1, "Because tainted bottles traced back to separate factories, the tampering most likely happened after the bottles reached shops."],
    [2, "How far did Johnson and Johnson's recall extend?", ["Only to the shops where poisoned bottles were found", "Only to the state of Illinois", "Across the entire United States, around 31 million bottles", "It did not recall the product at all"], 2, "Although the deaths were confined to the Chicago area, the company recalled capsules nationwide to remove any possible risk."],
    [3, "What did the relaunched Tylenol capsules introduce?", ["A cheaper price to win back customers", "A new brand name", "Child-proof caps for the first time", "Triple-sealed, tamper-evident packaging"], 3, "A glued box, neck seal and foil seal made tampering much easier to detect, and similar seals became standard."],
    [4, "What happened to James Lewis?", ["He was convicted of extortion but never charged with the murders", "He was convicted of all seven murders", "He was a Johnson and Johnson employee who was sacked", "He was never identified"], 0, "Lewis sent a ransom-style letter and was convicted of extortion, but the poisonings themselves remain officially unsolved."],
    [5, "Which principle is NOT one of the lessons usually drawn from the Tylenol case?", ["Act quickly before every fact is known", "Put public safety ahead of short-term profit", "Avoid speaking to the media until the crisis passes", "Fix the underlying weakness"], 2, "The company communicated openly with authorities and the media; hiding from them is the opposite of what made the response a model."]
  ],
  { added: "2026-10-26" });

WB.L("double-entry-bookkeeping", "business", "intermediate", "Double-entry bookkeeping: the Renaissance idea behind every business", "A friar who was friends with Leonardo da Vinci wrote down a method that still sits beneath every company's accounts, from corner cafes to global giants.",
  [
    ["Merchants who needed a better system", "Double-entry bookkeeping grew out of the busy trading cities of medieval Italy.",
      "Picture a merchant in fourteenth-century Genoa, Florence or Venice. He buys wool on credit, ships cloth to distant ports, borrows from partners and lends to customers. Keeping a simple list of money in and money out is no longer enough to know whether he is actually making a profit, who owes him and whom he owes. Over the late Middle Ages, Italian merchants and bankers gradually developed a cleverer way of recording business. Surviving account books show the method in use by at least the 1300s, and the great banking houses, including the Medici bank, relied on it. Nobody knows exactly who invented it. Like many useful tools, it seems to have evolved through practice long before anyone wrote a textbook."],
    ["Luca Pacioli writes it down", "In 1494 the friar and mathematician Luca Pacioli published the first widely read description of the method.",
      "Luca Pacioli was a Franciscan friar and mathematics teacher who moved in remarkable circles. He was a friend and collaborator of Leonardo da Vinci, who drew illustrations for another of Pacioli's books, on geometry and proportion. In 1494, in Venice, Pacioli published a huge mathematics textbook known as the Summa. Tucked inside was a section explaining how Venetian merchants kept their books. Thanks to the new printing press, it spread across Europe and was translated and copied for generations. That is why Pacioli is often called the father of accounting, even though he described a system rather than inventing it. His guidance was practical, stressing careful daily records and books that always balance."],
    ["Every transaction has two sides", "In double-entry, each transaction is recorded in at least two accounts, so the totals always stay in balance.",
      "The core idea is simple and powerful. Every business transaction affects at least two things, so you record it twice. Suppose a small business buys a delivery van for 20,000 dollars in cash. It has gained a van, but lost cash of the same amount. Both changes are written down. If the business instead borrows 20,000 dollars from a bank, it gains cash and also gains a debt to repay. Recording both sides means nothing slips through the cracks. Money never just appears or vanishes in the books; it always comes from somewhere and goes somewhere. This also creates a built-in error check, because if the two sides do not match, a mistake has been made."],
    ["The accounting equation", "Assets always equal liabilities plus owners' equity.",
      "Double-entry rests on a single balancing rule, known as the accounting equation: assets equal liabilities plus equity. Assets are what the business owns or controls, such as cash, stock, equipment and money customers owe it. Liabilities are what it owes to others, such as loans and unpaid bills. Equity is what is left for the owners, including the money they put in and the profits kept in the business. The equation simply says that everything a business has was funded either by borrowing or by its owners. Because every transaction is recorded on both sides, the equation stays true after each one. The balance sheet, one of the main financial reports of any company, is this equation set out in detail."],
    ["Debits and credits, demystified", "Debits and credits are simply the left and right sides of an account, not good and bad.",
      "The words debit and credit confuse almost everyone at first, because in everyday life a credit sounds good and a debit sounds bad. In bookkeeping they just mean the left side and the right side of an account. By convention, debits increase assets and expenses, while credits increase liabilities, equity and income. Every transaction must have equal debits and credits. Here is why your bank statement seems backwards: when you deposit money, the bank records a credit to your account, because from the bank's point of view your money is something it owes you, a liability. The language is written from the perspective of whoever keeps the books, which explains much of the muddle."],
    ["Why it still matters", "The system lets owners, lenders, investors and tax authorities trust and compare the numbers.",
      "More than five centuries after Pacioli, accounting software still runs on double-entry under the surface. When an app shows a small business owner a profit figure, a balance sheet or an amount owing for tax, it is producing those reports from paired entries. The method made larger, more complex businesses possible, because owners could track performance without personally watching every coin. It also made it possible for outsiders such as banks and investors to judge a business from its accounts. Some historians argue it helped shape modern capitalism itself, though that bigger claim is debated. The German writer Goethe had a character call it one of the finest inventions of the human mind. For anyone starting a business, understanding it is a genuine superpower."]
  ],
  [
    [1, "What role did Luca Pacioli play in the history of double-entry bookkeeping?", ["He invented the system from scratch", "He published the first widely read description of it in 1494", "He banned it in Venice", "He designed the first accounting software"], 1, "Merchants were already using double-entry; Pacioli's printed explanation spread it across Europe, earning him the title father of accounting."],
    [2, "A business buys a van for 20,000 dollars cash. How is this recorded in double-entry?", ["Only the drop in cash is recorded", "Only the new van is recorded", "The van is added as an asset and cash goes down by the same amount", "It is recorded as income"], 2, "Every transaction has two sides: here one asset (the van) goes up and another asset (cash) goes down by the same amount."],
    [3, "Which is the accounting equation?", ["Assets = liabilities + equity", "Profit = assets − equity", "Income = cash + debt", "Liabilities = assets + expenses"], 0, "Everything a business owns was funded either by borrowing (liabilities) or by its owners (equity), so the two sides always balance."],
    [4, "Why does a bank statement show your deposit as a credit?", ["Because credits always mean good news", "Because the bank is charging you a fee", "Because the deposit is treated as income for you", "Because, to the bank, your deposit is money it owes you, a liability"], 3, "Bookkeeping terms are written from the record-keeper's point of view; a credit increases the bank's liability to you."],
    [2, "What built-in benefit does recording both sides of every transaction give?", ["It reduces the tax a business pays", "It provides an error check, because the two sides must match", "It removes the need for receipts", "It makes every business profitable"], 1, "If total debits and credits do not match, a mistake has been made somewhere, which helps bookkeepers catch errors."]
  ],
  { added: "2026-10-26" });

WB.L("post-it-note-story", "business", "beginner", "The Post-it Note: a failed glue and a choir bookmark", "One of the world's best-known office products began as a glue that did not stick properly. It took a scientist's persistence and a singer's frustration to see its value.",
  [
    ["A company built on a mistake", "3M began as a mining venture that failed, then learned to survive by inventing.",
      "The company behind the Post-it Note has a habit of turning setbacks into products. It started in 1902 as the Minnesota Mining and Manufacturing Company, set up to mine a mineral the founders believed was valuable corundum, ideal for grinding wheels. It turned out to be a poorer quality rock that was not much use. To survive, the company moved into making sandpaper, and then into a long line of other products, including masking tape and Scotch tape. Along the way it developed a culture that encouraged employees to experiment. A long-running 3M practice, dating back to the late 1940s, lets technical staff spend a share of their working time, often described as fifteen per cent, on projects of their own choosing."],
    ["The glue that would not grip", "In 1968 the chemist Spencer Silver created an unusual adhesive that stuck lightly and peeled away cleanly.",
      "In 1968 a 3M research chemist named Spencer Silver was trying to develop a very strong adhesive, the kind that might be used in aircraft construction. Instead he produced something odd. His new glue formed tiny spheres that gripped surfaces only lightly, so objects could be stuck down, peeled away and stuck down again without leaving much residue. By the usual measure it was a failure, because it was not strong. Silver, however, was convinced it was special. He just could not work out what it was for. For years he gave talks and seminars inside the company, showing off the adhesive to anyone who would listen, and gained a reputation as a determined champion of a solution looking for a problem."],
    ["A problem in the choir", "Art Fry needed bookmarks that would stay put in his hymn book without damaging it.",
      "The breakthrough came from a colleague's annoyance. Art Fry, a 3M product development engineer, sang in his church choir in Minnesota. He marked the hymns in his book with scraps of paper, which kept falling out whenever he opened it. Around 1974 he remembered Spencer Silver's light adhesive and realised it could make the perfect bookmark: something that would stay in place but peel off without tearing the page. He began coating paper with the glue and experimenting. Soon he noticed an even bigger use. When he wrote a note on one of his sticky bookmarks and stuck it on a report for a colleague, it worked as a message that stayed attached without damaging the document underneath."],
    ["Why the notes are yellow", "The famous canary yellow colour came from scrap paper that happened to be nearby.",
      "Many famous product features were carefully designed. The colour of the Post-it Note was not. According to the company's own telling of the story, the team working on early versions used scrap paper that happened to be yellow, borrowed from a neighbouring laboratory. The colour stuck, in more ways than one, and canary yellow became the product's signature. It also turned out to be practical, because a bright yellow square stands out on a desk, a computer screen or a page of black and white text. Today the notes come in a rainbow of colours, sizes and shapes, but yellow remains the shade most people picture. It is a reminder that not every successful choice in business comes from planning."],
    ["A rocky launch", "Early test sales flopped until people were given samples and tried the notes for themselves.",
      "Even with a working product, success was not guaranteed. In 1977 3M test marketed the notes in four American cities under the name Press 'n Peel, and sales were disappointing. The problem was that customers had never seen anything like it and could not imagine why they needed it. Instead of giving up, the company changed tactics. In 1978 it flooded the city of Boise, Idaho, with free samples, an effort later nicknamed the Boise Blitz. Once office workers actually used the notes, they wanted more, and a large majority of those surveyed said they would buy them. Renamed Post-it Notes, the product was launched across the United States in 1980 and in other countries soon after."],
    ["Lessons from a sticky note", "Great products often need both an inventor and a person who finds the right problem.",
      "The Post-it story is a favourite in business and design courses because it shows how innovation often really works. It was not a single flash of genius. It needed a technology nobody had a use for, a scientist stubborn enough to keep promoting it, a colleague with a real everyday problem, and a company culture that allowed time for side projects. It also shows a marketing truth: a genuinely new product can fail if customers cannot picture using it, and letting people try it can be more persuasive than any advertisement. Silver and Fry both received recognition for the invention. Today Post-it Notes are sold in well over a hundred countries and are one of the most recognisable office products ever made."]
  ],
  [
    [1, "What was unusual about Spencer Silver's 1968 adhesive?", ["It was the strongest glue ever made", "It stuck lightly and could be peeled off and reused", "It only worked underwater", "It glowed in the dark"], 1, "Silver was aiming for a strong glue but created one that gripped lightly and peeled away cleanly, which nobody knew how to use at first."],
    [2, "What problem first led Art Fry to use the adhesive?", ["Labelling files in a filing cabinet", "Sealing envelopes", "Bookmarks falling out of his choir hymn book", "Sticking photos into albums"], 2, "Fry's paper bookmarks kept slipping out of his hymn book, so he used the light adhesive to make bookmarks that stayed put."],
    [3, "Why are classic Post-it Notes yellow?", ["Market research showed yellow sold best", "Yellow paper was the cheapest available", "The founder's favourite colour was yellow", "The early team happened to use yellow scrap paper from a nearby lab"], 3, "The colour was an accident of what scrap paper was at hand, though bright yellow proved practical because it stands out."],
    [4, "What turned around the product's disappointing test sales?", ["Giving out large numbers of free samples so people could try it", "Cutting the price in half", "Selling it only to schools", "Changing the glue to make it stronger"], 0, "In the 1978 Boise Blitz, people who actually used the notes wanted more; trying the product was more persuasive than explaining it."],
    [0, "How did 3M's company culture help the Post-it Note happen?", ["It required all inventions to be approved within a month", "It let technical staff spend some working time on projects of their own choosing", "It banned staff from sharing ideas between departments", "It only funded products that had already been sold elsewhere"], 1, "3M's long-running practice of allowing time for self-directed projects encouraged the kind of experimenting that produced the notes."]
  ],
  { added: "2026-10-26" });

WB.L("ikea-flat-pack", "business", "beginner", "IKEA and the genius of the flat pack", "A teenager selling pens by mail order in rural Sweden built the world's biggest furniture retailer. The secret involved taking the legs off a table.",
  [
    ["A teenager in Småland", "Ingvar Kamprad founded IKEA in 1943, when he was just seventeen.",
      "Ingvar Kamprad grew up on a farm called Elmtaryd near the village of Agunnaryd, in the Småland region of southern Sweden, an area known for poor, stony soil and thrifty people. As a boy he made money selling matches, then seeds, Christmas cards and pencils. In 1943, at the age of seventeen, he registered his own company. He named it by combining his initials with the first letters of his farm and village: Ingvar Kamprad, Elmtaryd, Agunnaryd, or IKEA. At first the business sold small items such as pens, wallets and picture frames by mail order. In the late 1940s Kamprad added furniture made by local manufacturers, and before long furniture had become the main business."],
    ["Taking the legs off a table", "In 1956 a staff member removed a table's legs to fit it in a car, and the flat pack idea took hold.",
      "The most famous moment in IKEA's history is a small one. In 1956 an IKEA employee and designer named Gillis Lundgren was trying to fit a table into a car after a photo shoot. It would not go in, so he took the legs off. The company realised that furniture designed to be packed flat would be much cheaper to store and ship, and less likely to be damaged on the way. Customers could carry it home themselves and assemble it with simple tools. Flat packing was not entirely new in furniture making, but IKEA made it the heart of its business. Lundgren went on to design many IKEA products, including the Billy bookcase, launched in 1979."],
    ["The boycott that backfired", "When Swedish rivals tried to shut IKEA out, it found new suppliers abroad and became even cheaper.",
      "IKEA's low prices made established Swedish furniture retailers furious. In the 1950s they pressured local manufacturers to stop supplying the upstart, and IKEA was even barred from some furniture trade fairs. Rather than give in, Kamprad looked further afield. In the early 1960s IKEA began having furniture made in Poland, where production costs were much lower. The boycott, intended to crush the company, pushed it into building its own international supply chain and designing more of its own products. This made IKEA cheaper still and gave it control over how its furniture was made and packed. It is a classic example of a business turning a threat into an advantage by changing its approach rather than its goal."],
    ["The customer does the work", "IKEA cuts costs by having shoppers pick up, transport and assemble their own furniture.",
      "IKEA's first store opened in the town of Älmhult in 1958, and it has been refined ever since. The model rests on a simple bargain: the customer does part of the work, and in return pays a lower price. In a traditional furniture shop, staff fetched items, the shop delivered them and assembled them. At IKEA, shoppers browse a showroom, write down product codes, collect flat boxes from a self-serve warehouse, take them home and build the furniture themselves. Even the winding one-way path through the showroom is designed to show customers as much of the range as possible. Restaurants, play areas and cheap snacks encourage families to stay longer, and longer visits tend to mean bigger purchases."],
    ["The IKEA effect", "Researchers found people value things more when they have built them themselves.",
      "Assembling furniture can be frustrating, but it may also make people like it more. In a study published in 2012, researchers Michael Norton, Daniel Mochon and Dan Ariely asked people to assemble simple IKEA storage boxes, fold origami or build Lego models. Those who had built the item were willing to pay noticeably more for it than people who were offered an identical item already assembled. The researchers called this the IKEA effect: labour leads to love, at least when the task is successfully completed. When participants failed to finish building something, the effect faded. Businesses use the idea widely, from build-your-own toy shops to apps that let you customise your profile."],
    ["Thrift as a strategy", "IKEA built a global empire by designing everything around low cost.",
      "Kamprad, who died in 2018, was famous for his personal frugality and spoke often about avoiding waste. Many critics noted that the frugal image sat alongside an enormous fortune, but thrift was undeniably built into the company's strategy. IKEA designers often start with a target price and then work out how to make a product that meets it, choosing materials, shapes and packaging to save money at every step. A bookcase that packs a few centimetres flatter means more fit on a truck, which lowers transport costs for every unit. Many products are named using a system based on Scandinavian words and place names. Today IKEA operates hundreds of stores in dozens of countries, including several in Australia."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What does the name IKEA stand for?", ["Ingvar Kamprad, Elmtaryd, Agunnaryd", "International Kitchen Equipment Association", "Ingvar's Kitchen and Ergonomic Accessories", "It is a Swedish word for home"], 0, "Kamprad combined his initials with the first letters of his family farm, Elmtaryd, and his village, Agunnaryd."],
    [1, "How did the flat pack idea take hold at IKEA in 1956?", ["A factory accident damaged a batch of legs", "A designer removed a table's legs so it would fit into a car", "Customers demanded cheaper delivery", "A government law limited furniture sizes"], 1, "Gillis Lundgren took the legs off a table to fit it in a car, and IKEA saw that flat furniture was cheaper to store and ship."],
    [2, "What was the result of Swedish rivals boycotting IKEA in the 1950s?", ["IKEA went bankrupt", "IKEA stopped selling furniture", "IKEA moved its headquarters to Poland", "IKEA found cheaper suppliers abroad and became more competitive"], 3, "Shut out by local manufacturers, IKEA had furniture made in Poland, lowering costs and giving it more control over production."],
    [4, "What is the IKEA effect?", ["The tendency for shoppers to buy more in large stores", "The tendency to value things more when we have built them ourselves", "The tendency for flat pack furniture to break", "The habit of getting lost in big showrooms"], 1, "Researchers found people paid more for items they had successfully assembled themselves than for identical pre-built ones."],
    [3, "What is the basic bargain at the heart of IKEA's store model?", ["Customers pay more for better service", "Staff assemble all furniture for free", "Customers do part of the work in exchange for lower prices", "Furniture can only be bought online"], 2, "Shoppers collect, transport and assemble their own furniture, which cuts IKEA's costs and keeps prices low."]
  ],
  { added: "2026-10-26" });

WB.L("pareto-principle", "business", "beginner", "The 80/20 rule: the vital few", "A few customers bring most of the sales, a few problems cause most of the headaches. The 80/20 rule is one of the most useful ideas in business, and one of the most misunderstood.",
  [
    ["An economist and the land of Italy", "Vilfredo Pareto noticed that wealth in Italy was concentrated in a small share of the population.",
      "Vilfredo Pareto was an Italian engineer turned economist, working in the late nineteenth and early twentieth centuries. Studying data on wealth and income, he noticed a striking pattern: a small fraction of people owned a very large share of the land and wealth. In Italy, roughly 80 per cent of the land was held by about 20 per cent of the population, and he found similar lopsided patterns in other countries. A popular story says he also noticed that a small number of pea pods in his garden produced most of the peas, though that tale is hard to verify. What Pareto really found was a mathematical pattern in how wealth was distributed, which economists still study today."],
    ["Joseph Juran gives it a name", "A quality expert turned Pareto's observation into a general rule for management.",
      "Pareto himself never proposed a universal 80/20 rule. That came decades later from Joseph Juran, a Romanian-born American engineer who became one of the most influential figures in quality management. Working on manufacturing defects in the mid twentieth century, Juran noticed that a small number of causes were responsible for most of the problems. He called these the vital few, as opposed to the trivial many, and later rephrased the second group as the useful many. Juran named the idea after Pareto, and the label stuck: the Pareto principle. He later admitted he had stretched Pareto's work beyond what Pareto himself had claimed, but the practical insight proved hugely valuable in factories, offices and beyond."],
    ["What the rule actually says", "In many situations, a minority of causes produces a majority of the effects.",
      "At its heart the Pareto principle is a simple observation: inputs and outputs are often unequal. A minority of causes frequently produces most of the results. In a typical business you might find that a small group of customers accounts for most of the revenue, a few products bring in most of the profit, or a handful of recurring faults generate most of the complaints. The numbers 80 and 20 are a memorable shorthand, not a law of nature. The real split might be 70/30, 90/10 or 95/5. And the two numbers do not have to add up to 100, because they measure different things: one the share of causes, the other the share of effects."],
    ["Using it at work", "The principle helps you focus effort where it will make the biggest difference.",
      "The practical power of the Pareto principle is in deciding where to look first. Quality engineers use a Pareto chart, a bar chart that sorts problems from most frequent to least, to show which few causes deserve attention. A cafe owner might discover that a handful of menu items make most of the money, and choose to simplify the rest. A support team might find that most calls are about a few common issues, and fix those with clearer instructions. In personal work, it suggests asking which tasks truly move a project forward, and doing those before polishing the details. The question is not how to do everything, but which few things matter most."],
    ["Where people get it wrong", "The 80/20 rule is a pattern to check, not a guarantee, and the trivial many still matter.",
      "Because it is catchy, the Pareto principle is often misused. Some people treat 80/20 as an exact law and assume the numbers without measuring anything. Others conclude that the remaining 80 per cent of customers, tasks or products can simply be dropped. That can be a serious mistake. Small customers may become large ones, cheap products may draw people into a store, and routine tasks such as paperwork, safety checks and maintenance can have serious consequences if neglected. Juran's later phrase, the useful many, was a deliberate correction. The principle is best used as a prompt to gather data and look for imbalances, not as an excuse to ignore everything outside the top fifth."],
    ["Why the world is lopsided", "Many systems where success builds on success naturally produce uneven outcomes.",
      "Why do so many things follow this lopsided pattern? One reason is that success often compounds. A popular product gets better shelf space, more reviews and more word of mouth, which makes it more popular still. A big customer may grow faster because it already has scale. In networks such as cities, websites and social media, a few hubs tend to attract a large share of the connections. Mathematicians describe such distributions as heavy-tailed or power laws, and they appear in fields from earthquakes to book sales. Understanding this helps explain why averages can mislead. When a few cases dominate the total, the typical case may look very different from the average one."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What did Vilfredo Pareto actually observe?", ["That 80 per cent of workers did 20 per cent of the work", "That a small share of the population owned most of the land and wealth", "That 20 per cent of products caused 80 per cent of defects", "That most businesses fail within five years"], 1, "Pareto noticed that wealth and land in Italy, and elsewhere, were concentrated among a small fraction of people."],
    [1, "Who turned Pareto's observation into a general management principle?", ["Henry Ford", "Adam Smith", "Joseph Juran", "Peter Drucker"], 2, "The quality expert Joseph Juran coined the idea of the vital few and the trivial many and named it after Pareto."],
    [2, "Why don't the two numbers in an 80/20 split need to add to 100?", ["They measure different things: share of causes and share of effects", "Because the rule is only approximate", "Because Pareto made an arithmetic error", "They must always add to 100"], 0, "One number is the share of causes and the other the share of results, so a 90/30 or 95/5 pattern is perfectly possible."],
    [3, "What does a Pareto chart show?", ["Profit over time", "The organisational structure of a company", "Prices compared with competitors", "Problems or causes sorted from most frequent to least"], 3, "Sorting causes by frequency makes it easy to see which few deserve attention first."],
    [4, "What is a common misuse of the 80/20 rule?", ["Using data to look for imbalances", "Assuming everything outside the top 20 per cent can safely be dropped", "Focusing first on the biggest problems", "Using it to set priorities"], 1, "Smaller customers, tasks and products can still matter a great deal, which is why Juran later called them the useful many."]
  ],
  { added: "2026-10-26" });

WB.L("psychological-safety", "business", "intermediate", "Psychological safety: why the best teams admit mistakes", "A researcher expected the best hospital teams to make the fewest errors. Her data said the opposite, and that surprise changed how we think about teamwork.",
  [
    ["A puzzling result", "Amy Edmondson found that better hospital teams reported more errors, not fewer.",
      "In the 1990s a young researcher named Amy Edmondson was studying medication errors in hospital teams. She expected to find that teams with better leadership and stronger relationships would make fewer mistakes. When the data came in, it seemed to show the reverse: the better teams were reporting more errors. At first this made no sense. Then Edmondson realised what might be going on. The best teams were probably not making more mistakes. They were more willing to talk about them. In teams where people feared blame, errors were more likely to be hidden. That insight led her to a concept that has become one of the most influential ideas in modern management: psychological safety."],
    ["What psychological safety means", "It is a shared belief that you will not be punished or humiliated for speaking up.",
      "Edmondson, who went on to become a professor at Harvard Business School, defines psychological safety as a shared belief in a team that it is safe to take interpersonal risks. In plain terms, people feel able to ask a question, admit a mistake, raise a concern or offer a half-formed idea without being embarrassed, ignored or punished. It is about the climate of the group, not one person's confidence. The same individual might speak freely in one team and stay silent in another. Psychological safety does not mean everyone agrees, or that work is always comfortable. It means honest disagreement and bad news can come to the surface early, while there is still time to act on them."],
    ["Google goes looking for the perfect team", "Google's Project Aristotle found that how a team works together mattered more than who was on it.",
      "In 2012 Google launched an internal research effort called Project Aristotle to work out what made some of its teams more effective than others. Researchers studied around 180 teams, expecting that the right mix of skills, personalities or backgrounds would explain success. Those factors turned out to matter far less than expected. What mattered most was how team members interacted. When Google published its findings in 2015, it listed five key dynamics: psychological safety, dependability, structure and clarity, meaning, and impact. Psychological safety was described as by far the most important of the five, the foundation the others depended on. The findings drew wide attention and helped bring Edmondson's work to a much larger audience."],
    ["Not about being nice", "Psychological safety works best when combined with high standards.",
      "A common misunderstanding is that psychological safety means lowering expectations or avoiding criticism. Edmondson is clear that it does not. She describes a simple grid. When safety is low and standards are low, people are apathetic. When safety is high but standards are low, people are comfortable but not stretched. When standards are high and safety is low, people become anxious and hide problems to protect themselves. The goal is the fourth box: high standards and high safety, which she calls the learning zone. There, people are pushed to do excellent work and feel able to say when something is going wrong. Candour and kindness are not opposites; good teams need both."],
    ["Why silence is dangerous", "Many disasters involve someone who noticed a problem but did not feel able to speak up.",
      "The cost of silence can be enormous. Investigations into accidents in aviation, healthcare and engineering have often found that someone saw a warning sign but hesitated to raise it with a more senior person. Aviation responded by developing crew resource management training from around the 1980s, which teaches junior crew to voice concerns clearly and captains to invite them. In business the costs are usually quieter but still real: a flawed product nobody challenged, a project that everyone privately doubted, a safety shortcut nobody reported. Fear does not stop problems happening. It mostly stops leaders hearing about them until they are much bigger and more expensive to fix."],
    ["Building it in your team", "Leaders build psychological safety by inviting input, admitting their own limits and responding well to bad news.",
      "Psychological safety can be built, and leaders have the biggest influence. Edmondson suggests three broad practices. First, frame the work as a learning problem rather than a simple execution problem, being honest that there is uncertainty and that everyone's input is needed. Second, acknowledge your own fallibility, with something as simple as saying you might be missing something. Third, model curiosity by asking genuine questions. Just as important is how you react when someone does speak up. Thanking a person for raising a problem, even unwelcome news, encourages others to do the same. Reacting with anger teaches the whole team to stay quiet. Trust is built slowly and can be damaged very quickly."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why did better hospital teams appear to report more errors in Edmondson's study?", ["They were more careless", "They were more willing to talk openly about mistakes", "They had more patients", "They used a different reporting system"], 1, "Edmondson concluded the stronger teams were not making more mistakes; they felt safer reporting them."],
    [1, "Which best describes psychological safety?", ["A shared belief that it is safe to take interpersonal risks such as admitting mistakes", "A guarantee that nobody will ever be criticised", "A personal trait of confident people", "A workplace safety regulation"], 0, "It is a property of the team's climate: people feel able to speak up, ask questions and admit errors without humiliation."],
    [2, "What did Google's Project Aristotle find mattered most for team effectiveness?", ["Having the smartest individual members", "Similar personalities", "Psychological safety", "Working in the same office"], 2, "Who was on the team mattered less than how members interacted, and psychological safety was the most important dynamic."],
    [3, "In Edmondson's grid, what happens with high standards but low psychological safety?", ["The learning zone", "The comfort zone", "Apathy", "Anxiety, with problems hidden"], 3, "Pushed hard but afraid to speak up, people protect themselves by hiding problems; the goal is high standards and high safety."],
    [5, "Which leader behaviour most builds psychological safety?", ["Thanking people who raise problems, even unwelcome ones", "Only sharing information once decisions are final", "Reacting firmly whenever someone admits an error", "Avoiding questions that might reveal uncertainty"], 0, "How leaders respond to bad news teaches the whole team whether speaking up is safe."]
  ],
  { added: "2026-10-26" });

WB.L("unit-economics", "business", "intermediate", "Unit economics: does each sale actually make money?", "A business can grow fast, win millions of customers and still be heading for ruin. Unit economics is the simple maths that tells you which kind of business you have.",
  [
    ["Zooming in on one sale", "Unit economics asks whether a single sale or customer is profitable on its own.",
      "Big company accounts can hide a lot. Revenue may be soaring while losses grow even faster. Unit economics cuts through this by asking a simple question: if you look at one unit, does the business make or lose money? The unit could be one cup of coffee, one delivery, one subscription or one customer. You compare the money that unit brings in with the costs it directly creates. If each unit loses money, growth does not save you; it just loses money faster. If each unit makes money, the remaining question is whether enough units can be sold to cover the fixed costs of running the business, such as rent, salaries and equipment."],
    ["Contribution margin", "Contribution margin is the price of a unit minus the costs that rise with each sale.",
      "Imagine a coffee cart selling coffee at five dollars a cup. The beans, milk, cup and lid cost about one dollar fifty, and card fees add a few cents more. Those are variable costs, because they rise with every extra cup sold. What is left, roughly three dollars twenty, is called the contribution margin. It is the amount each sale contributes towards the fixed costs and, eventually, profit. If the cart's fixed costs, such as permits, insurance and the owner's wage, come to 800 dollars a week, it needs to sell about 250 cups a week just to break even. That simple calculation shows instantly whether the plan is realistic."],
    ["The cost of winning a customer", "Customer acquisition cost is everything you spend to gain one new customer.",
      "For many businesses, especially subscriptions and online services, the biggest cost is not making the product but finding the customer. Customer acquisition cost, often shortened to CAC, is the total spent on marketing and sales in a period divided by the number of new customers gained. If a business spends 10,000 dollars on advertising and wins 200 customers, its acquisition cost is 50 dollars each. This number is easy to underestimate. Free trials, discounts, sales staff wages and referral rewards all count. Acquisition costs also tend to rise as a business grows, because the easiest and most enthusiastic customers are usually won first, and later ones take more effort and money to persuade."],
    ["What a customer is worth", "Customer lifetime value estimates the profit a customer brings over the whole relationship.",
      "The partner to acquisition cost is customer lifetime value, often shortened to LTV. It estimates how much profit, not just revenue, a typical customer generates over the whole time they stay. A simple version multiplies the margin per period by how many periods customers usually remain. A gym member who pays 20 dollars a week, of which 12 dollars is margin, and stays about a year on average, is worth roughly 600 dollars in margin. Investors often compare the two numbers. A common rule of thumb says lifetime value should be around three times acquisition cost or more, and that the acquisition cost should be earned back within a reasonable time. It is a guide, not a law."],
    ["A cautionary tale: MoviePass", "MoviePass grew rapidly by selling each customer more than it charged them.",
      "MoviePass is one of the best-known examples of broken unit economics. In 2017 the American service cut its subscription price to about ten US dollars a month, allowing members to see up to one movie in a cinema every day. The catch was that MoviePass paid cinemas close to the full ticket price for each film members watched. A single ticket could cost more than a month's subscription. Customers loved it, and membership soared into the millions within a year. But every enthusiastic moviegoer deepened the losses. The company tried restrictions, price changes and limits, which angered customers. The service shut down in 2019, and its parent company filed for bankruptcy in early 2020."],
    ["Asking the right questions", "Strong unit economics turn growth into an advantage instead of a threat.",
      "Unit economics is useful whether you run a market stall or evaluate a startup. The key questions are simple. What is the price, and what are the true variable costs of each sale? How much does it cost to win a customer, and how long do they stay? How long until a new customer pays back what it cost to win them? Some businesses deliberately lose money on early customers, expecting costs to fall with scale or customers to buy more later. Occasionally that works. Often it does not, and the plan rests on hope rather than evidence. The healthiest businesses can show that each unit makes money now, so that every extra sale makes the company stronger."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why can rapid growth be dangerous if unit economics are negative?", ["Growth always increases fixed costs", "If each unit loses money, selling more units just loses money faster", "Customers stop buying when a business grows", "Taxes rise with each customer"], 1, "When every sale costs more than it brings in, scaling up multiplies the losses instead of fixing them."],
    [1, "A product sells for 10 dollars and its variable costs are 4 dollars. What is its contribution margin?", ["14 dollars", "4 dollars", "10 dollars", "6 dollars"], 3, "Contribution margin is price minus variable costs: 10 minus 4 leaves 6 dollars toward fixed costs and profit."],
    [2, "A business spends 10,000 dollars on marketing and gains 200 new customers. What is its customer acquisition cost?", ["50 dollars", "200 dollars", "20 dollars", "2,000 dollars"], 0, "Dividing total acquisition spending by new customers gives 10,000 divided by 200, or 50 dollars per customer."],
    [3, "What does customer lifetime value estimate?", ["The total number of customers a business will ever have", "The price of the most expensive product", "The profit a typical customer brings over the whole relationship", "How long a business will survive"], 2, "Lifetime value estimates margin earned from a customer across the entire time they stay, not just one purchase."],
    [4, "What was the core flaw in MoviePass's model?", ["Its app was too hard to use", "Cinemas refused to accept its cards", "It charged far more than competitors", "It often paid cinemas more per member than members paid in subscriptions"], 3, "A single ticket could cost MoviePass more than a member's monthly fee, so the most active customers caused the biggest losses."]
  ],
  { added: "2026-10-26" });

WB.L("tupperware-brownie-wise", "business", "beginner", "Tupperware, Brownie Wise and the home party", "A clever plastic container was gathering dust on shop shelves. Then a single mother from Detroit worked out how to sell it, and changed direct selling for good.",
  [
    ["A chemist's plastic box", "Earl Tupper created lightweight containers with an airtight seal in the 1940s.",
      "Earl Tupper was an American inventor from New Hampshire with endless ideas and a restless streak. In the late 1930s he worked for the chemical company DuPont, where he became interested in polyethylene, a new plastic. The raw material was hard to work with, but Tupper developed ways to purify and mould it into containers that were light, unbreakable and flexible, unlike the brittle plastics of the time. His real breakthrough was the lid. Inspired by the lids of paint tins, he designed a seal that snapped on and could be made airtight by pressing out a little air, the famous burp. Tupperware went on sale after the Second World War, and it was genuinely innovative."],
    ["Great product, poor sales", "In shops, Tupperware sat on shelves because customers did not understand how the seal worked.",
      "Despite the clever design, Tupperware sold poorly in hardware and department stores. Part of the problem was that shoppers had never seen anything like it. Plastic was still a novelty, and the special seal was not obvious by looking at a container on a shelf. Many people could not work out how to close the lids properly, so the main selling point was lost. The product needed to be shown, explained and handled. At the same time, some saleswomen working for home-selling companies such as Stanley Home Products had begun buying Tupperware and demonstrating it at gatherings in people's homes. Their sales were far better than anything happening in shops."],
    ["Enter Brownie Wise", "Brownie Wise, a divorced single mother, became Tupperware's most successful seller through home parties.",
      "One of those sellers was Brownie Wise. Born in Georgia, she had divorced and was raising her son alone in Detroit, earning money through home demonstrations. She discovered Tupperware and found it sold brilliantly when women could see it, touch it and watch the lid seal. Wise built a network of dealers and moved to Florida, and her group's results were so strong that they caught the attention of Earl Tupper himself. In 1951 Tupper made a bold decision. He pulled Tupperware out of shops altogether and committed to selling it through home parties. He put Wise in charge of the new sales operation, Tupperware Home Parties, where she held the title of vice-president."],
    ["How a Tupperware party works", "The party combined demonstration, social connection and the trust of friends.",
      "The home party model was simple but powerful. A host invited friends and neighbours to her house. A dealer demonstrated the products, often with games and a playful show of how the seal kept food fresh or how a sealed bowl full of liquid could be tossed without spilling. Guests could handle everything and place orders, and the host received a gift for organising it. Buying from a friend in a relaxed social setting created a sense of trust and obligation that a shop counter could not match. For many women in the 1950s, who were largely excluded from business careers, becoming a dealer offered independent income, flexible hours and the chance to rise through a sales organisation."],
    ["Rewards, recognition and a sudden fall", "Wise built a culture of celebration, became a business celebrity, and was then forced out.",
      "Wise understood motivation. At the company's Florida headquarters she held lavish annual events, known as jubilees, where top sellers were celebrated and rewarded with prizes ranging from appliances to cars and boats. Recognition was as important as money. Sales grew rapidly, and in 1954 Wise became the first woman to appear on the cover of the American magazine Business Week. But her fame and independence led to clashes with Tupper, who reportedly resented her growing profile. In 1958 he dismissed her. That same year he sold the company to the Rexall Drug Company for about sixteen million US dollars. Wise received a modest payout, and for years her role was largely left out of the company's official history."],
    ["The legacy of the party plan", "The home party model spread to many other products and remains a key part of direct selling.",
      "Tupperware became a global brand, and the party plan was copied by companies selling cosmetics, kitchenware, candles, jewellery and much more. The model works because it combines product demonstration with personal relationships, though critics point out that the social pressure to buy from a friend can be uncomfortable. Times eventually changed. As more women worked outside the home and shopping moved online, the party model struggled, and Tupperware's sales declined for years. In 2024 the company filed for bankruptcy protection in the United States. Yet the core lesson remains sharp. A product that customers do not understand will not sell, however good it is. Finding the right way to show it can matter as much as inventing it."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What was the key innovation in Earl Tupper's containers?", ["They were made of glass", "They were the first containers sold online", "An airtight seal inspired by paint tin lids", "They kept food frozen without a freezer"], 2, "Tupper designed a snap-on lid that could be made airtight by pressing out air, the famous burp, inspired by paint tin lids."],
    [1, "Why did Tupperware sell poorly in shops at first?", ["Customers did not understand how the seal worked", "It was too expensive", "It was banned in department stores", "It came in only one size"], 0, "The seal was not obvious from a shelf display; the product needed demonstrating to show its value."],
    [2, "What decision did Earl Tupper make in 1951?", ["To sell the company to DuPont", "To pull Tupperware from shops and sell it through home parties", "To stop making plastic containers", "To fire Brownie Wise"], 1, "Impressed by home-party sales, Tupper withdrew the product from stores and put Brownie Wise in charge of the new sales operation."],
    [3, "Why did buying at a home party often work better than buying in a shop?", ["Party prices were always lower", "Products at parties were made of better plastic", "Shops refused to give refunds", "Guests could handle the product and buy in a trusted social setting"], 3, "Demonstration plus the trust of friends and neighbours made the products easier to understand and more persuasive to buy."],
    [4, "What milestone did Brownie Wise reach in 1954?", ["She became the first woman on the cover of Business Week", "She bought Tupperware from Earl Tupper", "She founded a rival company", "She was elected to the US Congress"], 0, "Wise's success made her a business celebrity, though she was dismissed by Tupper four years later."]
  ],
  { added: "2026-10-26" });

WB.L("valuing-a-business", "business", "advanced", "What is a business worth? Valuation explained", "Two experts can look at the same company and arrive at prices billions apart. Valuation is part maths, part judgement, and understanding it changes how you see every price tag in the market.",
  [
    ["Price versus value", "The price of a business is what someone pays; its value is what it can produce for its owners.",
      "Warren Buffett, a student of the investor Benjamin Graham, has summed it up neatly: price is what you pay, value is what you get. The price of a business is a fact, such as the latest share price or the amount agreed in a sale. Its value is an estimate of what the business is really worth to its owners, and estimates differ. A buyer and a seller may disagree sharply, both acting in good faith. The fundamental idea behind most serious valuation is that a business is worth the cash it will generate for its owners over its remaining life. Everything else, from complicated spreadsheets to rules of thumb, is a way of estimating that cash and deciding what it is worth today."],
    ["Money today beats money tomorrow", "Because of interest, risk and inflation, a dollar in the future is worth less than a dollar now.",
      "The foundation of valuation is the time value of money. Would you rather have 100 dollars today or 100 dollars in a year? Almost everyone chooses today, and for good reasons. Money in hand can be invested to earn a return, it is not exposed to the risk of a promise being broken, and inflation can erode what future money will buy. To compare money at different times, analysts discount future amounts back to the present. If you could earn 10 per cent a year elsewhere, then 100 dollars received in one year is worth about 90.91 dollars today, because 90.91 dollars invested at 10 per cent grows to 100. Money further in the future is discounted more heavily."],
    ["Discounted cash flow", "A discounted cash flow model adds up the present value of all expected future cash.",
      "The most thorough valuation method is called discounted cash flow, or DCF. An analyst forecasts the cash a business will generate in each future year, usually free cash flow, meaning cash left after running the business and making necessary investments. Each year's forecast is discounted back to today's dollars, and the results are added together. Because nobody can forecast precisely forever, analysts usually model the next five or ten years in detail and then estimate a single terminal value for everything after that. A common approach treats the business as growing at a steady rate forever: annual cash flow divided by the discount rate minus the growth rate. In many valuations, this terminal value makes up most of the total."],
    ["The discount rate and risk", "Riskier cash flows are discounted at higher rates, which makes them worth less today.",
      "The discount rate is where judgement and risk come in. It represents the return investors require for putting money into this business instead of somewhere else. A stable utility with predictable cash flows might be discounted at a lower rate, while a young technology company with uncertain prospects demands a higher one. Professionals often use a weighted average cost of capital, blending the cost of the company's debt with the return expected by its shareholders. Small changes have big effects. A business generating one million dollars a year, growing at 2 per cent forever, is worth 12.5 million dollars at a 10 per cent discount rate. At 9 per cent, the same business is worth about 14.3 million."],
    ["Valuation by comparison", "Multiples value a business by comparing it with similar businesses that have known prices.",
      "Discounted cash flow is rigorous but laborious, so analysts also use multiples. The idea is similar to valuing a house by checking recent sales of comparable houses nearby. The price to earnings ratio compares a company's share price with its profit per share. Enterprise value to EBITDA compares the value of the whole business, including its debt, with its earnings before interest, tax, depreciation and amortisation. If similar companies trade at ten times EBITDA, a business earning five million dollars might be valued around fifty million. Small private businesses are often valued as a multiple of the owner's profit. Multiples are quick and grounded in real market prices, but if the whole market is overpriced, they simply copy the error."],
    ["Enterprise value versus equity value", "The value of the whole business differs from the value belonging to its shareholders.",
      "A frequent source of confusion is the difference between what a business is worth and what its owners' shares are worth. Enterprise value is the value of the entire operating business, regardless of how it is funded. Equity value is what belongs to the shareholders after the claims of lenders are settled. Roughly, equity value equals enterprise value minus net debt, meaning debt minus spare cash. Picture buying a house worth 800,000 dollars with a 500,000 dollar mortgage attached: the house is worth 800,000, but your equity is only 300,000. That is why two companies with identical operations can have very different share values. One may carry heavy debt, leaving much less for shareholders."],
    ["Garbage in, garbage out", "A valuation is only as good as its assumptions, so test them rather than trust a single number.",
      "Valuation models look precise, with numbers calculated to the last dollar, but they rest on forecasts about growth, margins and risk that nobody can know for certain. As the earlier example showed, changing the discount rate by one percentage point can shift a valuation by more than ten per cent, and changing the long-term growth rate has similar effects. Careful analysts therefore present a range rather than a single figure, test what happens under pessimistic and optimistic scenarios, and check their answer against multiples. Many experienced investors also insist on a margin of safety, another idea associated with Graham: only buying when the price is comfortably below their estimate of value, so that errors in their assumptions do not ruin them."]
  ],
  [
    [1, "If you can earn 10 per cent a year, roughly what is 100 dollars received in one year worth today?", ["110 dollars", "About 90.91 dollars", "100 dollars", "About 80 dollars"], 1, "90.91 dollars invested at 10 per cent grows to about 100 dollars in a year, so that is its present value."],
    [2, "In a discounted cash flow valuation, what is the terminal value?", ["The price at which the business was founded", "The value of the company's buildings", "An estimate of the value of all cash flows beyond the detailed forecast period", "The amount owed to lenders"], 2, "Analysts forecast a few years in detail, then estimate one terminal value for everything after, which often makes up most of the total."],
    [3, "What happens to a valuation when a higher discount rate is used?", ["The valuation falls, because future cash is worth less today", "The valuation rises", "Nothing changes", "The business becomes less risky"], 0, "A higher rate reflects more risk or better alternatives, so future cash is discounted more heavily and is worth less now."],
    [5, "A business has an enterprise value of 50 million dollars and net debt of 20 million. What is its approximate equity value?", ["70 million dollars", "50 million dollars", "20 million dollars", "30 million dollars"], 3, "Equity value is roughly enterprise value minus net debt: 50 minus 20 leaves about 30 million for shareholders."],
    [4, "What is a key weakness of valuing a business using multiples?", ["It cannot be used for private businesses", "If comparable companies are overpriced, the valuation copies the error", "It ignores the company's earnings entirely", "It requires forecasting cash flows forever"], 1, "Multiples rely on market prices of similar businesses, so a market-wide bubble or slump flows straight into the result."]
  ],
  { added: "2026-10-26" });
